Well, the reason most people don't have that much trouble with
starvation with a pacifist is because most people who try it in the
early game are either healers or (I think) tourists. Healers are
poison resistant, so they can eat the kobold corpses the pet won't.
(Realizing this helped my pacifist healers get to minetown a lot more
easily). Tourist start off with food, so they have time.

Since you're trying a gnomish wizard, you might want to try not doing
the protection racket. IIRC you don't start out with much money
anyway, so you just have to hope for shops to rob, and robbing in
minetown is dangerous, since by that time all the monsters will be a
lot bigger than you. You'll get some money from selling your books,
but I'm not sure how much.

If you want to do the protection racket:
-Hurry. Your pet needs to grow, yes, but you are on a timer. You don't
have to kill everything on each level, just get through it. It's
easier for a healer who can check his pet is big enough to advance,
but practice hurrying anyway. You'll also need to hurry *out* of the
mines and up once you buy protection. You'll gain levels very quickly,
and creatures will grow accordingly, so it's easier to hurry out and
descend again slowly.
-IIRC, wizards start out with two random rings. If one is slow
digestion, you're good to go. If it's poison resistance you can do the
kobold trick (just make sure they don't come from kobold zombies). I
haven't been poisoned in a while, but I *think* the only effect from
eating something posionous is lowering your stength, so a ring of
sustain ability could work too. Check on it. Just remember that rings
make you hungry faster, so only put them on while eating (unless slow
digestion of course)
-Other people mentioned how to please your god. Prayer will help you,
so follow their advice.
-Casting spells usually makes you hungry. Just so you know.

My suggestion would be, for a wizard, check if you have the above
rings. If you don't, just don't bother with the protection racket.
Otherwise, start going down and if you don't have enough money by the
time you see mine's entrance, start killing and leveling up, it's not
worth it.

I haven't played wizards that much, just healers and rangers, so I
have experience with the protection racket but not with what strategy
works best for a wizard.

Good luck.

